<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; The Holy Trinity" cic="CCC 232-267" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">For use after class or in a group. Print this page or work in your notebook.</p>

<h3>Remembering the essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>What does it mean that the Trinity is the &laquo;central mystery of the Christian faith&raquo;? Why is it not just an advanced topic?</li>
<li>What are the three divine Persons and what is the relationship between them?</li>
<li>What does &laquo;God is Love&raquo; (1 Jn 4:8) mean for understanding the Trinity?</li>
<li>In the baptism of Jesus (Mt 3:16-17), how do the three Persons manifest themselves?</li>
<li>What is the difference between the &laquo;missions&raquo; of the Trinity and the &laquo;opera ad extra&raquo; (the external works of God)?</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>For reflection and writing</h3>

<p>Answer each question honestly. There are no &laquo;correct&raquo; answers to these personal questions.</p>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. If God is a communion of love from all eternity, what does that tell us about the meaning of our human relationships?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Think about your family, your friends, your community. Do they live as communion or as individualism?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">2. Is it harder for you to imagine God as solitude or as communion? Which image of God feels closer to you?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Many would imagine God alone in heaven. The Christian faith says he has never been alone: he has always been Father, Son, and Holy Spirit.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">3. In what moment of your life have you experienced something like Trinitarian love: love that gives itself, that does not hold back, that generates life?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">It could be in your family, in a friendship, in an act of service, in the Eucharist.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">4. The sign of the cross plunges us into the Trinity every time we make it. How can you live with more awareness of that mystery in your daily life?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Name a concrete action: pray the Glory Be with attention, make the sign of the cross slowly, thank each divine Person.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">5. St. Paul blesses with &laquo;The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ, the love of God, and the fellowship of the Holy Spirit&raquo; (2 Cor 13:13). What does it mean for you to live under that blessing?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Grace (Son), love (Father), and fellowship (Spirit) &mdash; the three dimensions of Trinitarian life.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content" id="activities">

<h3>Practical Activities</h3>

<h4>A. Draw Rublev's Icon</h4>
<p>Look up Andrei Rublev's icon of the Trinity online. Observe it carefully: the three angels, the chalice in the center, the empty space in front of them. Write down what strikes you and why you think there is an empty place at the table.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>B. Pray with the Trinity</h4>
<p>This week, when you pray, pause to address each Person: &laquo;Father, I thank you for... Son, I thank you for... Holy Spirit, I thank you for...&raquo; Write your Trinitarian prayer.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>C. Observe Trinitarian love in life</h4>
<p>During the week, write down three moments when you experienced love that gives itself without holding back: a person who served you, a gesture that moved you, a moment of communion.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Verse to memorize</h3>

<div class="bible-passage">
<p>&laquo;The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ, the love of God, and the fellowship of the Holy Spirit be with you all.&raquo;</p>
<p class="bible-passage__ref">&mdash; 2 Corinthians 13:13</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Prayer for this week</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>Glory to the Father, and to the Son, and to the Holy Spirit,<br />
God, one and triune,<br />
communion of love from all eternity.<br />
Father, who created me by love;<br />
Son, who redeemed me by your cross;<br />
Holy Spirit, who dwells in me;<br />
grant that I may live in communion with you and with others,<br />
that I may never seek the solitude of selfishness,<br />
but the joy of shared love.<br />
Amen.</p>
</blockquote>

</section>

